{
  "dom_id": "step-q315628",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})+4\\,(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
